## 🧠 Key Concepts Applied

- **Packet structure:** every IP packet has a source and destination address, and (for TCP/UDP) source and destination ports identifying the specific service/connection
- **Protocol identification:** distinguishing TCP vs UDP traffic by inspecting which layer is present in the captured packet
- **Live capture vs static analysis:** unlike reading a saved `.pcap` file, this script processes packets in real time as they arrive on the interface

This builds directly on the packet-level understanding developed in my earlier [Wireshark packet analysis writeups](../wireshark-analysis) — the same TCP/IP concepts (source/destination IP, ports, protocol) are here implemented programmatically instead of viewed through a GUI.

## 🔐 Relevance to Cyber Security

Understanding how to build a basic sniffer highlights exactly what a defender needs to detect at the network level:
- Unexpected destination IPs or ports could indicate malware communicating with a command-and-control server
- Unusual protocol usage on a given port can be a sign of tunneling or protocol misuse
- This is the same foundational skill that underlies commercial network monitoring and intrusion detection tools

